Z||000013||DaRkLoRD||12-03-2000||01:55 AM||||slightly off-topic, but here goes...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>I wish there was a way to use my home radio setup in a car.. I'm from a fairly rural area, and we get a grand total of <B>2</B> radio stations, on a good day, with most radios.<BR>Last fall I designed a new kind of radio antenna, and finally found a radio receiver that would work with it this summer. (I had to find one where I could easily disconnect the FM antenna and attach a wire) I ended up getting more radio stations than I could count... and at awesome quality reception.. it actually doesn't have an antenna per se... and according to conventional wisdom, it shouldn't work.. but it does... it works better than I'd ever expected... so, when I first hooked it up and heard a toronto radio station... I yelled out "suck on that, Marconi!!!!!!" (even though marconi didn't invent radio, he based a lot of his designs on the earlier work of nikola tesla..)<BR>it was cool.. my parents didn't understand how it worked, but they acknowledged that it did... the "antenna" was underground... and only 3 feet long. I used a shielded coaxial cable to connect it to the radio, and the signal cut out when I unhooked the wire from the antenna... I'm going to build a better one when I'm home for the summer, and I'll take pictures this time.<P>I've heard that long ago, some radio experimenters used a technique like this as well.. I just built my own, based on vague reports from 60+ years ago.<P>------------------<BR>   <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>   <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A><p>[This message has been edited by DaRkLoRD (edited 12-03-2000).]||||reg||6
