Z||000018||Raydar||12-03-2000||09:00 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DaRkLoRD:<BR><B>slightly off-topic, but here goes...     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Last fall I designed a new kind of radio antenna, ..the "antenna" was underground... and only 3 feet long. I used a shielded coaxial cable to connect it to the radio...<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE> <P>Darklord,<BR>Is that all there is to it? I'm guessing it's more complicated than it sounds. <BR>I'd like to try to build one as well. (Maybe a loonnnng one for AM, so I can listen to those ugly talk-radio programs.)   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"> <BR>Antenna design is fascinating to me, although I've been away from radio (except microwave) for a while. <BR>Any info that you can share?<BR>Thanks!<P>Oh yeah. I listen to WGST, 640 AM (Talk radio.), 99X, and Z93 for oldies.<P>Steve<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 12-03-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||DaRkLoRD||12-03-2000||10:24 PM||||Raydar, with the radio I used, the AM antenna was separate from the FM one.. I never bother with AM anyway, all the good music is on FM. =)<P>I knew I was onto something when I listened to a concert on CBC, and then turned the dial a bit and was listening to a french program from Quebec.. big change from before, when I got a local station (FM102 from Wingham) and FM106.5, which wasn't clear at all..<P>After I'm done my final exams for the semester, I'll write you an email about the details of the setup.. it was really easy to do, the hardest part was finding a suitable receiver. my sister found me one at a garage sale for $2.00...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000020||sqoach||12-03-2000||10:28 PM||||wut's a radio...station...???<P>hehe.  i know what it is, but i hardly ever listen to the radio. i bought the cd player, might as well get some good use outta it  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>sQoaCh<BR>fiero7@hotmail.com<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mysig_1.JPG"> ||||reg||10
Z||000021||Raydar||12-04-2000||10:31 AM||||Darklord,<BR>Thanks! That'll be great!<P>I'm thinking about using an ordinary stereo receiver. Has screw lugs for the FM (and separate ones for the AM) antenna. I live at the bottom of a hill, and my shop is in the basement. Radio reception really bites it. ||||reg||14
Z||000022||Songman||12-04-2000||03:05 PM||||My favorite station is any that will play my music!<P>Actually, I am pretty partial to WSM AM 650 in Nashville because you can still get real country music there. And 103.3 in Nashville is starting to play more of it now...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000023||DaRkLoRD||12-04-2000||08:01 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>Darklord,<BR>Thanks! That'll be great!<P>I'm thinking about using an ordinary stereo receiver. Has screw lugs for the FM (and separate ones for the AM) antenna. I live at the bottom of a hill, and my shop is in the basement. Radio reception really bites it. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>As long as you're able to run a wire outside, it should be easy. Luckily when we had our air conditioning unit put in 2 summers ago, the hole that the guys drilled wasn't completely sealed.. so I was able to get the wire outside through that hole.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>You might want to run the cable through a window first to test it before putting it through a wall into your shop, as placement of the antenna is important. (can't be too close to buried electrical wires etc)<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
